**Problem.** Compute $\Delta^*\Delta_*\mathcal{O}_X$ for the diagonal embedding $\Delta:X\to X\times X$ of a variety $X$.

**Answer:** $\Delta^*\Delta_*\mathcal{O}_X \cong \mathcal{O}_X$.

**Proof.**

Since $X$ is a variety (hence separated), the diagonal morphism
$$\Delta:X\hookrightarrow X\times X$$
is a closed immersion. We use the following standard fact.

**Lemma.** For any closed immersion $i:Z\hookrightarrow Y$ and any $\mathcal{O}_Z$-module $\mathcal{F}$, there is a natural isomorphism
$$i^*i_*\mathcal{F}\;\cong\;\mathcal{F}.$$

*Proof of Lemma.* By definition of pullback,
$$i^*i_*\mathcal{F} \;=\; \mathcal{O}_Z\otimes_{i^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y} i^{-1}(i_*\mathcal{F}).$$
Because $i$ is a closed immersion (in particular a monomorphism), the unit of the $(i^{-1},i_*)$ adjunction is an isomorphism, giving
$$i^{-1}(i_*\mathcal{F})\;\cong\;\mathcal{F}$$
as $i^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y$-modules. Concretely, $i_*\mathcal{F}$ is the sheaf on $Y$ obtained by extending $\mathcal{F}$ by zero outside $Z$, and applying $i^{-1}$ recovers $\mathcal{F}$ on $Z$.

The $\mathcal{O}_Z$-module structure on $\mathcal{F}$ is defined via the surjective ring map $i^\sharp:i^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{O}_Z$; that is, the $i^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y$-action on $\mathcal{F}$ factors through $\mathcal{O}_Z$. Therefore
$$\mathcal{O}_Z\otimes_{i^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y}\mathcal{F}\;\cong\;\mathcal{O}_Z\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_Z}\mathcal{F}\;\cong\;\mathcal{F},$$
where the first isomorphism uses that $\mathcal{F}$ is already an $\mathcal{O}_Z$-module (so tensoring over $i^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y$ with $\mathcal{O}_Z$ just "restricts scalars back" to $\mathcal{O}_Z$, which changes nothing). This proves the Lemma. $\square$

Applying the Lemma with $i=\Delta$, $Z=X$, $Y=X\times X$, and $\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_X$, we obtain
$$\boxed{\Delta^*\Delta_*\mathcal{O}_X\;\cong\;\mathcal{O}_X.}$$

**Remark (context).** This is the *underived* computation. The result reflects that $\Delta$ is a closed immersion, so $\Delta_*$ is fully faithful with left inverse $\Delta^*$. The derived analogue $\mathbf{L}\Delta^*\Delta_*\mathcal{O}_X$ is far richer — it computes the Hochschild homology $\bigoplus_i\wedge^i\mathbb{L}_X[-i]$ (via the HKR isomorphism when $X$ is smooth) — but the non-derived pullback collapses all of this to $\mathcal{O}_X$ in degree $0$.

